<header class="page-head"><p class="kicker">Developers</p><h1>Link protocol</h1><p class="lead">The binary protocol between the radio and a PC, on the cable and over Bluetooth.</p></header>
<nav class="devnav" aria-label="Developer pages"><a href="/dev/">Overview</a> <a href="/dev/building/">Building</a> <a href="/dev/link-protocol/" class=on aria-current=page>Link protocol</a> <a href="/dev/update-file/">Update file</a> <a href="/dev/codeplug/">Codeplug format</a> <a href="/dev/terminal-pages/">Terminal pages</a> <a href="/dev/flash-map.html">SPI flash map</a></nav>
<div class="prose">

<p>The binary protocol between the radio and a PC (Ferrite Console, <code>ferrite_link.py</code>), on the programming cable and over Bluetooth. Its rules are chapter 29 of the manual (LINK-1 and on); this is Appendix G, the wire format.</p>
<p>The wire format of chapter 29 (LINK-1, LINK-21 – LINK-23), as built Oct 6, 2026; protocol 3 since decision 333 (Nov 1, 2026: 2-byte zone numbers, the zone record's count, <code>USED</code> in parts, the raw data messages and the layouts; LINK-59 – LINK-62). Shared code: <code>ferrite-core</code> <code>link.rs</code>; the radio's side: <code>ferrite-app</code> <code>link.rs</code>; a PC reference: <code>ferrite/tools/ferrite_link.py</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Frames.</strong> A frame on the wire is its body COBS-encoded, then a 0x00 delimiter (0x00 never appears inside). The body is <code>type</code>, <code>seq</code>, <code>payload</code> (up to 240 bytes), then the CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE (poly 0x1021, initial 0xFFFF) of type, seq and payload, little-endian. The PC sends a 0x00 first to take the cable (TERM-2). On Bluetooth the same bytes go over the BT513's FFE0 service, characteristic FFE1: writes (without response) of at most 20 bytes, paced for the module's 9600-baud UART (about 0.8 KB/s), and the answers as notifications in any chunking; FF31 is never used. The cable and Bluetooth each have their own session (LINK-28); a write while the other port's session is mid-write answers <code>busy</code>. Every request (type 0x01–0x7F) is answered with <code>type | 0x80</code>, the same <code>seq</code>, and a payload that starts with a status byte. Numbers are little-endian.</p>
