<th>Layout</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1 settings</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>The settings record (<code>ferrite-app</code> <code>persist.rs</code>, the <code>Settings</code> fields in order, the flag byte, then the power-on message), under 512 bytes; the PIN reads as zeros (32 spare bytes, once the approval tokens, are zeros too); protected fields kept on write (LINK-21)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2 channel</td>
<td>0–3999</td>
<td>number (u16), zone (u16, 0xFFFF none; one byte, 255 none, before protocol 3), RX (u32, 10 Hz), name (16 bytes, 0-padded, printable ASCII; characters outside the name set are written as <code>-</code>, CHAN-31), the slot settings (mode, narrow, RX tone, TX tone, offset, power, step, squelch, busy, reverse and split flags), flags (skip 1, APRS 2, RX only 4), end tone and tail (1 byte as record byte 31, TONE-17; a writer may leave it off: Global): 41 bytes (40 before protocol 3). A zone with its 256 channels already (CHAN-35): <code>invalid</code> "zone: full (256 channels at most)"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3 zone</td>
<td>0–511 (the next one creates)</td>
<td>name (16 bytes), scan all, shown, by distance, resume, dwell, a reserved byte (0; it was smart scan), priority channels (2 × u16, 0xFFFF none), then (protocol 3) its channel count (u16, read only: a write may leave it off, and one sent is ignored; CHAN-36): 28 bytes read, 26 or 28 written</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4 range preset</td>
<td>0–15 (the next one creates)</td>
<td>name (12 bytes), start, end (u32, 10 Hz), step, mode, narrow, resume (0 Global, 1–10 s, 11 carrier drop, 12 stop on hit; a record written without it reads as Global); its skips stay the radio's</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5 APRS settings</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>frequency (u16, 5 kHz units; 0 the region's), beacon mode (0 off, 1 manual, 2-9 every 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 60 min, 10-16 every 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10 km, 17 smart; bit 7 paused, the mode kept), power (0 low, 1 mid, 2 high), path (0 none, 1 WIDE1-1, 2 WIDE1-1,WIDE2-1, 3 custom, only once one is set), digipeater (bit 0 on, bit 1 my callsign too, bits 4-6 the wait 0-5 s), beacon after voice (0, 1), SSID (0-15), custom path (16 bytes, 0-padded), FX.25 on transmit (0 off, 16, 32, 64 check bytes; APRS-45), then (decision 347) the message sound (an index into the chimes, APRS-6.1), symbol (APRS-47), TX delay (10-100, 10 ms units, APRS-48), TX level (0-2, APRS-49), the comment (40 bytes, printable ASCII, 0-padded, APRS-50), ambiguity (0-4, APRS-51), Mic-E (0 off, 1-7, APRS-52), telemetry (0, 1, APRS-54): 72 bytes, written whole and checked as MENU 0 5's editors check (LINK-43; a 25- or 24-byte record keeps the radio's values past its end)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6 contact</td>
<td>0–1015, in callsign order (APRS-37)</td>
<td>callsign (10 bytes, 0-padded), name (12 bytes, 0-padded), flags (bit 0: delete). <code>WRITE_MANY</code> adds or renames by callsign whatever the index (use 0xFFFF) and deletes with bit 0; a full book answers <code>invalid</code> "1016 contacts"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7 channel position</td>
<td>0–3999, the channel's number</td>
<td>latitude, longitude (i32 each, little-endian, degrees x 1e7, north and east positive): 8 bytes, as the channel record stores them (CHAN-2). <code>READ_MANY</code> lists only the channels that have one (23 to an answer); <code>WRITE_MANY</code> sets it, or clears it with a record of length 0; out of range (latitude beyond ±90, longitude beyond ±180) answers <code>invalid</code>, another length <code>bad argument</code>; no channel at that number: nothing changes. <code>USED</code> of it: <code>bad argument</code>. Only from radios whose <code>HELLO</code> says 64 (LINK-53)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8 quick message</td>
<td>0–8 (TEXT-7)</td>
<td>flags (u8: 1 the owner's own text; 0 the factory text), then the text (0-67 bytes). <code>READ_MANY</code> lists all nine, with the text the radio uses. <code>WRITE_MANY</code> with flag 1 sets the text (printable, no vertical bar, tilde or open brace; empty stays empty), with flag 0 puts the factory text back (any text sent is ignored); a bad text answers <code>invalid</code>, an empty record <code>bad argument</code>, index 9 or more <code>not found</code> (LINK-54)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>9 waypoint</td>
<td>0–2031, newest first (GPS-10)</td>
<td>name (16 bytes, 0-padded, printable ASCII, at least 1), latitude, longitude (i32, degrees × 1e7), source (u8: 0 marked, 1 APRS, 2 link, 3 grid): 25 bytes. <code>WRITE_MANY</code> at an index in the list replaces that waypoint in its place; at 0xFFFF adds one as the newest (<code>invalid</code> "2032 waypoints" when full); a record of length 0 deletes the one at that index. Each record is applied after the one before it is stored, so indexes are the list as the earlier records left it: delete from the highest index down, then replace, then add. Bad name, position or source: <code>invalid</code> and the reason; another length <code>bad argument</code>; an index past the list <code>not found</code> (LINK-55)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>10 DTMF memory</td>
<td>0–15 (DTMF-12)</td>
<td>the symbols (length u8, then 1-16 of <code>0-9 A-D * #</code> as ASCII; lower case is taken as capitals), then the name (the rest: up to 10 printable bytes; none for no name). <code>READ_MANY</code> lists only the memories set. <code>WRITE_MANY</code> sets both, writing each only when it changed; a record of length 0 clears the memory and its name; bad symbols or name: <code>invalid</code> and the reason (LINK-56)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>11 range preset skips</td>
<td>0–15, the preset's index (as 4)</td>
<td>the skipped frequencies (u32 each, 10 Hz), 0-48 of them (SCAN-10), blank entries left out (LINK-58.1). <code>READ_MANY</code> lists the presets that have any. <code>WRITE_MANY</code> replaces the list (length 0 clears it); a frequency outside the preset's start and end, or twice, answers <code>invalid</code> and the reason; a length not a multiple of 4 <code>bad argument</code>; no preset at that index <code>not found</code>. The list is kept in the preset's record: it stays with its preset when another is deleted, and goes with it when it is (LINK-58)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>12 satellite</td>
<td>0–31, the place (SAT-2)</td>
<td>status (u8: 0 active, 1 inactive, 2 unknown), flags (u8: 1 half duplex to start, 2 Doppler on the 70 cm side only to start), transponders used (u8, 0-5), 0, NORAD catalogue number (u32), name (16 bytes, printable ASCII, NUL-padded, at least 1), the elements: epoch (u32, seconds since 2000-01-01 00:00 UTC; u16 milliseconds; 2 bytes 0), inclination, RAAN, argument of perigee, mean anomaly (u32 each, 1e-7 degree), eccentricity (u32, 1e-7), mean motion (u32, 1e-8 revolution a day), B* (f32), 12 bytes 0; then 5 transponders of 16 bytes: uplink (u32, 10 Hz; 0 receive only), downlink (u32, 10 Hz), access tone, arming tone (u16 each, 0.1 Hz; 0 none), kind (u8: 0 voice, 1 APRS, 2 beacon), 3 bytes 0 (unused ones all 0): 152 bytes (<code>ferrite-core</code> <code>sat::wire</code>). <code>READ_MANY</code> lists the places held. <code>WRITE_MANY</code> puts a satellite in its place (its choices, trim, duplex, transponder and power, stay with its catalogue number); length 0 deletes the one there; length 0 at 0xFFFF deletes them all (one rewrite of the region, so the list written after it only programs erased bytes: Console writes a whole list as that, then places 0, 1, ... in order). A transponder outside 144–148 or 430–440 MHz (SAT-13), more than 5, a bad name, status, kind or elements: <code>invalid</code> and the reason; another length <code>bad argument</code>; <code>busy</code> while the record before is still being written (send it again). Only from radios whose second capability byte says 4 (LINK-63)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>13 satellites' info</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>declination at the radio (i16, 0.1 degree, east positive; 0x7FFF none, SAT-18), 2 bytes 0, the satellite data's build time (u32, UTC seconds: <code>sats.json</code>'s <code>built</code>), when Console wrote it (u32, UTC seconds), 4 bytes 0: 16 bytes. <code>READ_MANY</code> gives it when one is kept; <code>WRITE_MANY</code> writes it, length 0 clears it; a declination past ±180° <code>invalid</code> (LINK-64, SAT-21)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>14 radio record</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>Watch (u8, 0 1 1: 0 off, 1 A+B, 2 A+B+C), Battery Save (u8, 0 7 7 1: 0 off, 1-3), the GPS fix (u8: 0 none, 2 2D, 3 3D; read only), the message sound (u8, 0 7 5 5: an index into the chimes), latitude, longitude (i32 each, degrees × 1e7, north and east positive: the fix, or the fixed position 0 6 9 when there is none and it is on, GPS-3; read only), the fix's age (u16, seconds; 0xFFFF none): 14 bytes (LINK-66, decision 347). <code>READ</code> and <code>WRITE</code> (index 0); a write takes Watch and Battery Save, and the message sound when it is 4 bytes or more, each as the menu changes it.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Event log records</strong> (<code>LOG_READ</code>, LINK-42; the log screen's fields, INBOX-6): sequence number (u32), age (u32, seconds before the answer: the radio's clock has no date before GPS or a hand-set time, so the PC dates it from its own), source (u8: 0 APRS, 1 Pager, 2 SAME, 3 DSC, 4 AIS, 5 DTMF, 6 Selcall, 7 GPS), slot (u8), flags (u8: 1 unread, 2 a position follows), frequency (u32, 10 Hz), ID (u32), x (u16), y (u16): 23 bytes; then latitude and longitude (i32 each, degrees x 1e7) when flag 2 is set; then the name (length u8, up to 20 bytes) and the text (length u8, up to 64 bytes). At most 117 bytes, so an answer holds at least two. What ID, x, y, name and text hold depends on the source:</p>
