<p>The layout header (20 bytes at 0x000000, the rest of its sector erased): magic (u32 0x54525246, the bytes <code>46 52 52 54</code>), layout (u16: 3), 10 installer bytes (erased, 0xFF; bytes 6-9 later hold the calibration sector's CRC-32, which the firmware programs into them, CAL-7), CRC-32 (u32) of bytes 0-15 with bytes 6-9 taken as 0xFF. Layout 2 had the same header at 0x02C000 with layout 2. The radio writes it, never Console (raw writes to it are refused): the format, or the begin before a power-on's first raw write (decision 339).</p>
<p>The migration mark (decision 339; 16-byte records at 0x00E000, 256 places, raw writes refused): magic (u32 0x564D5246, the bytes <code>46 52 4D 56</code>), the layout found (u16), this layout (u16: 3), 4 bytes erased, CRC-32 of bytes 0-11. Before the first raw write of a power-on the radio programs one into the first erased place (none left: the sector erased first) unless a valid one is there, then the layout 3 header at 0x000000 unless a good one is there (that sector erased first if it holds anything else), each read back. A valid mark means a move not stamped: the radio waits for Console (UPD-16) whatever the header says, and says the chip's layout as 255. <code>STAMP</code> checks the header and clears the mark, programming its 16 bytes to zero (no erase), taken only once the store has nothing queued or being written. So the chip always holds the old header, or the mark, or (stamped) the new header and all the data.</p>
<p>The channel record (64 bytes; channel n at 0x010000 + (n / 63) × 4096 + (n % 63) × 64; each sector's last 64 bytes free): 0 RX (u32, 10 Hz; 0xFFFFFFFF: empty), 4 TX (u32: 0 the same as RX; else the absolute TX frequency, a split when flag bit 10 is set), 8 mode (0 FM, 1 NFM, 2 AM, 3-5 HF modes, CHAN-23, CHAN-26), 9 narrow (0, 1), 10 power + 1 (0 default), 11 step + 1 (0 the band's), 12 RX tone (u16, CHAN-3), 14 TX tone (u16), 16 flags (u16: bits 0 skip, 1 APRS, 2 RX only, 3 busy lockout, 5 talkaround, 6 hidden, 7 has position, 8 priority, 9 reverse, 10 split; 4 and 11-15 kept as they are), 18 the zone's low byte, 19 squelch (0 Global, 1-10 levels 0-9), 20 latitude and 24 longitude (i32, 1e-7 degree; 0x7FFFFFFF none), 28-30 kept, 31 end tone (bits 0-2) and tail (bits 3-4), 32 name (16 bytes, 0-padded), 48-59 kept (48 is 0 in a new record, 49-59 0xFF), 60 version (2), 61 the zone's high byte, 62 CRC-16 (u16) of bytes 0-61. The zone is u16 0-511, 0xFFFF none. It holds a channel when the RX isn't 0xFFFFFFFF, the CRC agrees, byte 60 is 2 and the zone is 0-511 or 0xFFFF. Version 1 (layout 2): byte 60 1, byte 61 0xFF, the zone the one byte 18 (0-253, 255 none).</p>
<p>The zone record (32 bytes; zone z at 0x050000 + (z / 127) × 4096 + (z % 127) × 32): 0 name (16, 0-padded), 16 flags (bit 0 scan all, 1 shown in ◀▶, 2 sort by distance), 17 resume (0 Global, 1-10 s, 11 carrier drop, 12 stop on hit), 18 dwell, 19 0, 20 priority 1 and 22 priority 2 (u16 channel numbers, 0xFFFF none), 24 the channel count (u16, CHAN-36; 0xFFFF in layout 2), 26-29 0xFF, 30 CRC-16 of bytes 0-29. Empty when bytes 0-29 are all 0xFF or the CRC disagrees. Zones exist from 0 up to the last record held (a deleted earlier one stays as an empty, unnamed zone).</p>
<p>Layout 2 to 3 (Console, from its copy of 0x02C000-0x08CFFF and layout 2's scratch ring 0x1E0000-0x1FFFFF). First, layout 2's own recovery: a scratch sector whose last 32 bytes (at offset 4064) start with a valid footer (magic u32 0x52435346, the target address u32, the CRC-32 of the sector's first 4064 bytes, a CRC-16 of those 12 footer bytes) has its 4064 bytes put over its target sector in the copy. Then, region by region (each layout 3 region erased whole, then its bytes programmed; 64-byte pieces that are all 0xFF can be skipped):</p>
