<h3 id="msg"><code>msg</code></h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Name</th>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>HELLO</code></td>
<td><code>0x01</code></td>
<td>Start a session: protocol version, then the client's name. The answer's third byte says whether the session may go on (1) or must send <code>AUTH</code> first (0: the radio's PIN is set and this is Bluetooth, LINK-30).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>INFO</code></td>
<td><code>0x02</code></td>
<td>Firmware version and what the radio holds.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>AUTH</code></td>
<td><code>0x04</code></td>
<td>The radio's PIN as ASCII digits (4-6), on Bluetooth while one is set (LINK-30, LINK-31): Ok, <code>Refused</code> (wrong; it counts toward the radio's lockout, PIN-3), or <code>Busy</code> (locked out: wait).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>READ</code></td>
<td><code>0x10</code></td>
<td>Read part of a record: what, index, offset, length.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WRITE</code></td>
<td><code>0x11</code></td>
<td>Write part of a record: what, index, offset, total length, bytes; applied when the last part arrives.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DELETE</code></td>
<td><code>0x12</code></td>
<td>Delete a channel, zone or preset.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BEGIN</code></td>
<td><code>0x13</code></td>
<td>A write session of N records starts: the radio shows its progress (LINK-9).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>END</code></td>
<td><code>0x14</code></td>
<td>The write session ends.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>USED</code></td>
<td><code>0x15</code></td>
<td>Which indexes hold a record (LINK-39): what; the answer is the index count (u16), then a bitmap, index i at bit <code>i % 8</code> of byte <code>i / 8</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>READ_MANY</code></td>
<td><code>0x17</code></td>
<td>Whole records from an index on (LINK-40): what, first index (u16), count (u16). The answer is the next index to ask from (u16), then each record held, as many as fit: index (u16), length (u8), the record; empty indexes are skipped.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WRITE_MANY</code></td>
<td><code>0x18</code></td>
<td>Several whole records in one frame (LINK-41): what, then each record as <code>READ_MANY</code> gives them (index, length, record). Applied in order, each as a <code>WRITE</code> of it would be; the answer's first byte after the status is how many were applied (a refusal is the next one's).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>STREAM</code></td>
<td><code>0x19</code></td>
<td>One request, its answers back to back (LINK-50, decision 285): the payload is another request's type (<code>READ_MANY</code>, <code>HEARD</code>, <code>MSGS</code>, <code>LOG_READ</code> or <code>UI</code>) and its payload. The radio answers with that request's answers in turn, each as its own frame, the first with this request's seq and each next one seq + 1 (so a frame lost on the way shows as a gap, and the PC asks again from the last it has): <code>STREAM_MORE</code> (&#124; ANSWER) while more follow, <code>STREAM</code> (&#124; ANSWER) for the last, each payload exactly the inner request's answer would be. Frames go only while the port has room, so the stream is flow-controlled by the link itself; any new request from the PC ends it. <code>bad argument</code> for another type. Only from radios that say <code>caps::STREAM</code> in <code>HELLO</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>STREAM_MORE</code></td>
<td><code>0x1A</code></td>
<td>A <code>STREAM</code> answer that more follow (radio to PC only, with <code>super::ANSWER</code>).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>LOG_READ</code></td>
<td><code>0x40</code></td>
<td>The event log, newest first (LINK-42): before (u32: records with a lower sequence number; 0xFFFFFFFF from the newest), then optionally the most to send (u8, 0: as many as fit). The answer: the next sequence number (u32), the records held (u16), how many follow (u8), then each (<code>LOG_FIXED</code> bytes, the position if it has one, then the name and the text, each a length and the bytes).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SLOT</code></td>
<td><code>0x16</code></td>
<td>A slot's working fields, read or changed as the keypad would (LINK-33): the slot (0-2), then optionally a <code>super::slot</code> mask and its fields in bit order. The answer is the slot as it is after.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BAUD</code></td>
<td><code>0x20</code></td>
<td>Propose a faster cable rate (LINK-11).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>REBOOT</code></td>
<td><code>0x21</code></td>
<td>Restart into the bootloader, for a firmware update (LINK-24): the cable, or a Bluetooth session that may go on (LINK-30); refused while transmitting. The answer goes out first, then the radio saves, goes quiet and resets.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>CMD</code></td>
<td><code>0x31</code></td>
<td>A terminal command line (LINK-34, chapter 37): run as the terminal's command line runs it. The answer is its output: a byte, 1 if more follows (ask with <code>MORE</code>), then text, lines ending in <code>\n</code>; or <code>invalid</code> and the terminal's refusal. A new <code>CMD</code> ends one still running.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MORE</code></td>
<td><code>0x32</code></td>
<td>The running command's next output, as <code>CMD</code>'s answer; 1 with no text: the radio is still working on it, ask again shortly. Nothing running: 0 and no text.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>KEY</code></td>
<td><code>0x30</code></td>
<td>A key on the radio (LINK-19, LINK-20): the key, then what it does (<code>super::key</code>). The keypad and side keys by stock's key code with a press type (LINK-19), the knob (<code>super::key::KNOB</code>), or remote PTT (<code>super::Ptt</code>, LINK-20); anything else answers <code>bad argument</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>UI</code></td>
<td><code>0x33</code></td>
<td>What the screen shows (LINK-19's readback, decision 150): as <code>CMD</code> <code>ui</code>, its text in lines (the screen, the status bar, the home cards or the list's rows, the hint bar, a toast), <code>MORE</code> for the rest. An optional byte: 0 the state, 1 the key names (<code>ui keys</code>).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SUBSCRIBE</code></td>
<td><code>0x50</code></td>
<td>Events this session wants pushed (LINK-46): a <code>super::event</code> mask (0 ends them), then optionally the UI's least gap in 100 ms units and flags (<code>super::event::RENEW</code>). Answer: the mask, the Live View dictionary's version, the next UI sequence number.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>EVENT</code></td>
<td><code>0x51</code></td>
<td>Radio to PC only, unasked: a frame of this type (below 0x80: the radio sends no requests, so it is never an answer) with the session's own event counter as its seq, the payload an <code>super::event</code> kind and its bytes (LINK-46). Sent by the PC as a request: answered <code>unknown type</code> as any other.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>HEARD</code></td>
<td><code>0x52</code></td>
<td>The APRS heard list (LINK-44): from an index (u8); the count, then as many stations as fit.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MSGS</code></td>
<td><code>0x53</code></td>
<td>APRS messages, in and out, newest first (LINK-44): from an index (u16); the count (u16), then as many as fit.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MSG_SEND</code></td>
<td><code>0x54</code></td>
<td>Send an APRS message (LINK-45): to (length, text), the message (length, text); queued as the radio's compose queues it.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MSG_STOP</code></td>
<td><code>0x55</code></td>
<td>Stop sending one of our APRS messages (LINK-49, APRS-13.1): to (length, text), the message (length, text); the newest of ours to that station with that text still being tried stops, kept as cancelled.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MSG_DELETE</code></td>
<td><code>0x59</code></td>
<td>Delete one APRS message (LINK-52, APRS-46.1): flags (1: one to us), the station (length, text: from, or to), the message (length, text); the newest that matches goes, as the radio's own Delete message.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>AUDIO</code></td>
<td><code>0x56</code></td>
<td>Receive audio to the PC (LINK-13.1, LINK-13.4): the cable only; payload one byte, 1 to listen (12 kHz µ-law, decision 330), 0 to stop (2 or 3: the bench's raw stream at 12 or 16 kHz, LINK-13.9). The answer carries the stream's rate (u16) and a block's samples (u16). Listening ends with the session (a <code>HELLO</code>, the session going quiet, a new connection): never on by itself (LINK-13.5).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>AUDIO_DATA</code></td>
<td><code>0x57</code></td>
<td>A block of receive audio, pushed unasked while listening (<code>crate::rxaudio</code>): seq (u16), the slot heard (0xFF none), flags (1: squelch open), then the µ-law codes, a byte a sample; with no codes, a keep-alive while nothing is heard. Never compressed (LINK-51), nor counted there.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>AUDIO_RAW</code></td>
<td><code>0x58</code></td>
<td>A block of the bench's raw receive audio (LINK-13.9, <code>AUDIO</code> 2 or 3 in a bench build; <code>crate::rawaudio</code>): seq (u16), the first sample's index (u32), the slot heard, flags, then 152 samples of 12 bits, two in three bytes. Pushed unasked, every block whatever the squelch; never compressed, as <code>AUDIO_DATA</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>STOCK_RESTORE</code></td>
<td><code>0x60</code></td>
<td>The way back to stock (BACKUP-7, LINK-4, LINK-8; <code>crate::restore</code>): the cable only. <code>START</code> stops the radio and hands the cable to the restore until it resets; erase, program and CRC requests follow, then <code>FINISH</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DATA_READ</code></td>
<td><code>0x61</code></td>
<td>LINK-59 (decision 333): the SPI flash's data range read raw, bytes or a CRC (<code>super::data</code>): Console's backup before an update moves data to another layout.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DATA_WRITE</code></td>
<td><code>0x62</code></td>
<td>LINK-60: the data range written raw, a sector erased or bytes programmed, and the layout header stamped last (<code>super::data</code>): the cable only.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>CLOCK</code></td>
<td><code>0x63</code></td>
<td>The radio's clock (LINK-65, GPS-23): with a payload, UTC seconds (u32) set it as 0 6 6 does by hand (GPS time takes over at the next fix while 0 6 6 says "from GPS"); empty, it is read. The answer either way: UTC seconds now (u32, 0 while the radio has no time), then its source (u8: 0 none, 1 GPS, 2 set by hand or the link). Only from radios that say <code>super::caps2::SATS</code>.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
