<h3 id="what"><code>what</code></h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Name</th>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>SETTINGS</code></td>
<td><code>1</code></td>
<td>The radio-wide settings, one record (index 0).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>CHANNEL</code></td>
<td><code>2</code></td>
<td>A channel by number, 0-999.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>ZONE</code></td>
<td><code>3</code></td>
<td>A zone by number.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>PRESET</code></td>
<td><code>4</code></td>
<td>A range preset by number.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>APRS</code></td>
<td><code>5</code></td>
<td>The APRS settings, one record (index 0; LINK-43): MENU 0 5's that aren't in the settings record.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>CONTACT</code></td>
<td><code>6</code></td>
<td>An APRS contact, in callsign order (APRS-42): written by callsign whatever the index.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>POSITION</code></td>
<td><code>7</code></td>
<td>A channel's position by channel number, 0-999 (LINK-53, decision 325): latitude and longitude (i32 each, little-endian, 1e-7 degree, north and east positive), 8 bytes. <code>READ_MANY</code> gives only the channels that have one; a <code>WRITE_MANY</code> record of length 0 clears it. Only from radios that say <code>super::caps::POSITION</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>QUICK</code></td>
<td><code>8</code></td>
<td>A quick message, 0-8 (LINK-54, TEXT-7): flags (bit 0 <code>super::QUICK_OWN</code>: the owner's own text; clear: the factory text), then the text (0-67 bytes). Only from radios that say <code>super::caps::LISTS</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WAYPOINT</code></td>
<td><code>9</code></td>
<td>A waypoint, newest first, 0-255 (LINK-55, GPS-10): name (16 bytes, 0-padded), latitude and longitude (i32 each, 1e-7 degree), source (u8: 0 marked, 1 APRS, 2 link, 3 grid): <code>super::WAYPOINT_LEN</code> bytes. A <code>WRITE_MANY</code> record replaces the waypoint at its index, adds one at <code>super::ADD</code>, deletes with length 0.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DTMF</code></td>
<td><code>10</code></td>
<td>A DTMF memory, 0-15 (LINK-56, DTMF-12): the symbols (length u8, then 1-16 of <code>0-9 A-D * #</code>), then the name (the rest, 0-10 bytes). Only the memories set are listed; a record of length 0 clears one.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>PRESET_SKIPS</code></td>
<td><code>11</code></td>
<td>A range preset's skipped frequencies (LINK-58, SCAN-10), by the preset's index (as <code>PRESET</code>): u32 each, 10 Hz, 0-48 of them. <code>READ_MANY</code> lists the presets that have any; a <code>WRITE_MANY</code> record replaces the list (length 0 clears it). Only from radios that say <code>super::caps2::PRESET_SKIPS</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SAT</code></td>
<td><code>12</code></td>
<td>A satellite, 0-31 (LINK-63, SAT-2): the record of <code>ferrite_core::sat::wire</code> (<code>super::SAT_LEN</code> bytes: status, flags, transponders used, catalogue number, name, elements, five transponders). <code>READ_MANY</code> lists the ones held; a <code>WRITE_MANY</code> record replaces the satellite at its index (the per-satellite choices stay with its catalogue number), length 0 deletes it, and length 0 at <code>super::ADD</code> deletes them all (one rewrite of the region, so the whole list's writes after it only program erased bytes). A transponder outside 144-148 or 430-440 MHz is refused (<code>invalid</code>, SAT-13).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SAT_INFO</code></td>
<td><code>13</code></td>
<td>The satellites' info, index 0 (LINK-64, SAT-18, SAT-21): declination (i16, 0.1 degree, east positive, 0x7FFF none), 2 bytes 0, the satellite data's build time (u32, UTC seconds), when Console wrote it (u32), 4 bytes 0: <code>super::SAT_INFO_LEN</code> bytes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>RADIO</code></td>
<td><code>14</code></td>
<td>The radio's own state the settings record doesn't hold, index 0 (LINK-66, decision 347): Watch (0 1 1: 0-2), Battery Save (0 7 7 1: 0-3), the GPS fix (0 none, 2 2D, 3 3D; read only), the message sound (0 7 5 5: an index into <code>tonegen::CHIMES</code>), latitude and longitude (i32 each, 1e-7 degree, north and east positive; the fixed position when there is no fix and it is on, GPS-3), the fix's age in seconds (u16, 0xFFFF none): <code>super::RADIO_LEN</code> bytes. A write takes Watch and Battery Save, and the message sound when it is 4 bytes or more, each as the menu changes it; the fix and position are ignored. Only from radios that say <code>super::caps2::RADIO</code>.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
